            <ArticleTitle>Diazepam for relief of irrigation pain after transurethral resection of the prostate.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>197-200</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Irrigation of the bladder after transurethral resection of the prostate is often distressing because of pain arising from detrusor muscle spasm. The effect of post-operative oral diazepam was compared with sacral epidural (caudal) block at the time of surgery, both treatments together and a control group, assessed by three-point rank scoring. Either treatment significantly reduced the incidence of pain reported by patients, P &lt; 0.001. No patient experienced pain after both treatments.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
